      passage:
        "<b>Returning the Tide</b><br><br>After three autumn storms flooded Harbor Lane, town planners examined a low field behind the road. The field had once been a salt marsh, but an earthen embankment built decades earlier separated it from tidal water. The marsh and the embankment had changed the shoreline, yet <u>it no longer slowed water moving toward nearby buildings</u><b>[1]</b>. Rather than raise the road again, the town approved a project to restore the marsh's natural movement of water.<br><br>Crews removed two sections of the embankment, <u>the incoming tide began filling the field twice each day</u><b>[2]</b>. They excavated branching channels and graded shallow terraces at several elevations. <b>[4]</b> Workers then <u>planted</u><b>[3]</b> bands of cordgrass, rushes, and salt-tolerant shrubs across the prepared ground. Within two growing seasons, roots held loose sediment along the channel edges.<br><br>During storms, the dense stems did not stop the tide. Instead, they <u>destroyed</u><b>[5]</b> some of the water's energy before it reached the road. Surveys found that flooding along the restored shoreline was shallower <u>than the unrestored shoreline</u><b>[6]</b>. The channels also became feeding areas for small fish, while birds nested on the higher terraces.<br><br><u>Consequently</u><b>[7]</b>, the early results did not make the project self-sustaining. Planners still measure plant survival, channel depth, sediment buildup, and the reach of storm tides. If water levels rise faster than the marsh surface, crews may need to add sediment or establish new vegetation at higher elevations.<br><br>The restored marsh is not a barrier that can be completed and ignored. <u>Its value lies in changing with the shoreline while being observed closely enough for the town to respond</u><b>[8]</b>.",
