      imageSources: [],
      passage:
        "<b>Reworking the Corner Wall</b><br><br>On a Saturday morning, artist Lena Cho pressed strips of blue tape against a blank wall beside the Mercer Avenue bus stop. The tape marked the edges of a mural planned by neighborhood residents. <u>Looking across the intersection, the wall's lowest section seemed likely to be blocked by parked delivery trucks</u><b>[1]</b>. Cho therefore shifted the main images upward before any paint was mixed.<br><br>The first design showed a detailed street map stretching across the wall, <u>several residents argued that the narrow lines would be difficult to see from passing buses</u><b>[2]</b>. Others wanted the map retained because it showed the locations of former shops and gathering places. To test both concerns, the artists painted two sample panels on plywood and placed <u>them</u><b>[3]</b> at the bus stop, where viewers compared the designs from the sidewalk and across the street.<br><br><u>For example</u><b>[4]</b>, the larger map proved legible at a distance, but it left little room for the handwritten memories residents had collected. The group eventually chose a simpler street grid bordered by short quotations. <b>[5]</b> Cho also replaced several delicate painted lines with ceramic tiles that could withstand rain and repeated cleaning.<br><br>Before installation, volunteers sorted the quotations by subject rather than by speaker. Phrases about work, food, transportation, and neighborhood change appeared beside the locations they described. One proposed quotation was <u>completely removed and taken out altogether</u><b>[6]</b> because no one could verify its source. Another was shortened to fit a tile without altering its meaning.<br><br>When the mural opened, the finished wall did not reproduce the original drawing exactly. That difference was not treated as a failure. <u>The project had gone through a bunch of changes because people kept having opinions</u><b>[7]</b>. <u>Instead, the completed mural recorded both the neighborhood's history and the decisions required to make that history visible in a public place</u><b>[8]</b>.",
